Subject: compose: drop the panel-origin lookup no profile can answer

DeriveWorkArea read screen.left and screen.top, with .value_or(0) behind each,
to offset the work area by the panel's origin. Neither key exists.
config/profile.schema.json declares width, height, the four avail_* fields, the
four avail_inset_* fields, color_depth, color_gamut, hdr, is_extended and
displays under screen, and sets additionalProperties false, so a profile that
carried screen.left would fail validation before the compositor ever saw it.
Both lookups could only ever miss, and both fallbacks could only ever be zero.

The comment above them was right about the measurement and wrong about where it
lands: the Windows reference capture did report availLeft 1920 on a second
monitor with a zero inset, but a profile expresses that through
screen.displays[].left, which DeriveWorkArea does not touch. The single-panel
path this function serves has its origin at zero by construction.

So the reads go and the arithmetic says what it does. This changes no emitted
value: origin_left and origin_top were zero on every input the schema admits.

Found by scripts/check-schema-wiring.py, which reconciles the schema against the
C++ that reads it in both directions. This was the reverse direction - C++
reading a key the schema does not define - and it is the cheap half of that
defect, because the expensive half is a key a profile can set and nothing reads.

Compile-gated at V1: base/apostate/compose.cc compiles against the pinned
macos-arm64 build.

Ledger: screen.availLeft, screen.availTop.

--- a/base/apostate/compose.cc
+++ b/base/apostate/compose.cc
@@ -1378,13 +1378,15 @@
 
   // availLeft/availTop are panel-relative: the Windows reference capture ran
   // on a second monitor at x=1920 and reported availLeft 1920 with a zero
-  // inset. So the inset is added to the panel origin, which is zero unless the
-  // panel states one.
-  const int origin_left = screen->FindInt("left").value_or(0);
-  const int origin_top = screen->FindInt("top").value_or(0);
-
-  screen->Set("avail_left", origin_left + *inset_left);
-  screen->Set("avail_top", origin_top + *inset_top);
+  // inset. So the inset is added to the panel origin, and the panel origin is
+  // always zero here because no profile can state one. This used to read
+  // "screen.left" and "screen.top" with a zero fallback, and
+  // config/profile.schema.json declares neither and forbids additional
+  // properties under screen, so both lookups could only ever miss. A profile
+  // with more than one display carries its origins per display in
+  // screen.displays[].left and .top, which this function does not touch.
+  screen->Set("avail_left", *inset_left);
+  screen->Set("avail_top", *inset_top);
   screen->Set("avail_width", *width - *inset_left - *inset_right);
   screen->Set("avail_height", *height - *inset_top - *inset_bottom);
 }
